== Canonicalization decisions ==

* '''Knowledge''' — public concept; do not equate with information or data.
* '''Belief''' — philosophical/cognitive concept; do not equate with faith or uncertainty.
* '''Epistemology''' — public philosophical field; BITwiki's internal epistemics is an application, not the definition.
* '''Evidence''' — general epistemic/scientific concept; internal BITwiki evidence policy remains separate.
* '''Empirical evidence''' — evidence grounded in observation/experiment/measurement; not synonymous with all evidence.
* '''Justification''' — canonical title is broad; Core must specify epistemic justification to distinguish from moral/legal justification.
* '''Uncertainty''' — broad public concept with separate epistemic, aleatory, measurement, model, semantic, and other uses.
* '''Epistemic status''' — use cautiously as a family term for epistemically evaluative properties/standings; do not present BITwiki's six labels as universal philosophy.
* '''Provenance''' — public lineage/origin concept spanning history, archives, data, science, and information systems.
* '''Verification''' and '''Validation''' — separate pages because their relation is domain-dependent and routinely confused.
* '''Falsifiability''' — property of a statement/system relative to possible counterevidence; distinguish logical falsifiability from practical falsification.
* '''Scientific method''' — pluralistic account; reject a schoolbook universal step sequence as the definition.
* '''Scientific model''' — representation used in scientific inquiry; distinguish model from theory, simulation, and data.
* '''Measurement''' — public science/metrology concept; preserve philosophical and metrological perspectives.
* '''Accuracy and precision''' — one comparison page because the public confusion is relational; use VIM/NIST distinctions.
* '''Reproducibility''' and '''Replication''' — separate pages; explicitly note that terminology varies by field.
* '''Explanation''' — general concept with strong scientific-explanation coverage, not only science.
* '''Counterexample''' — logic/mathematics/philosophy concept; distinguish from anecdote and merely contrary example.

== Cluster A — epistemic core ==

=== Knowledge ===

'''Capture definition:''' epistemic state/achievement ordinarily involving truth and belief, but no single reductive analysis is universally accepted.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* propositional knowledge ("knowing that") vs knowledge-how vs acquaintance;
* knowledge ≠ belief;
* knowledge ≠ justified true belief by simple definition;
* truth condition versus justification/warrant/reliability/anti-luck conditions;
* Gettier problem.

'''High-value sources:'''
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "The Analysis of Knowledge", substantive revision 2026: https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/knowledge-analysis/
* Edmund L. Gettier, "Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?", ''Analysis'' 23(6), 121–123 (1963). DOI: 10.1093/analys/23.6.121.
* Wikipedia/other encyclopedias for terminology/history/navigation only.

=== Belief ===

'''Capture definition:''' an attitude of taking a proposition/state of affairs to be true or the case. Belief can be dispositional rather than occurrent.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* belief ≠ knowledge;
* belief ≠ credence/degree of belief, though related;
* belief ≠ acceptance;
* belief ≠ faith;
* proposition/content vs psychological state;
* representational, dispositional, interpretationist/instrumentalist approaches.

'''High-value sources:'''
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Belief": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/belief/
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Formal Representations of Belief": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/formal-belief/
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Bayesian Epistemology": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/epistemology-bayesian/

=== Epistemology ===

'''Capture definition:''' philosophical study of knowledge, belief, justification, evidence, rationality, ignorance, skepticism, and related epistemic norms/properties.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* epistemology ≠ ontology;
* descriptive psychology of belief ≠ normative epistemology;
* individual/social/formal epistemology;
* internalism/externalism;
* foundationalism/coherentism/reliabilism/evidentialism as major families, not exhaustive taxonomy.

'''High-value sources:'''
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Epistemology" and specialized entries on internalism/externalism, foundationalism, coherentism, reliabilism.
* https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justep-intext/
* https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justep-foundational/
* https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justep-coherence/
* https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reliabilism/

=== Evidence ===

'''Capture definition:''' information, experience, observation, fact, reason, or other epistemically relevant material that bears on what is reasonable/justified to believe or on the support of a claim/hypothesis. Philosophical theories disagree about what counts as evidence and how it supports claims.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* evidence ≠ proof;
* evidence ≠ truth;
* evidence ≠ source prestige;
* total evidence and defeaters;
* evidence for a person vs public/scientific evidence;
* likelihood/support relations versus mere data possession.

'''High-value sources:'''
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Evidence": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/evidence/
* BITwiki internal evidence framework as local application only: [[BITwiki:Evidence]].
* Evidence should later link to [[Empirical evidence]], [[Justification]], [[Belief]], [[Hypothesis]], [[Causality]], [[Provenance]], and [[Uncertainty]].

=== Empirical evidence ===

'''Capture definition:''' evidential material derived from observation, experiment, measurement, or experience of the world rather than solely from formal deduction or conceptual analysis.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* empirical evidence ≠ all evidence;
* observation may be theory/model/instrument mediated;
* data ≠ evidence without relevance/inference context;
* observational evidence ≠ causal identification;
* empirical result ≠ interpretation.

'''High-value sources:'''
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Theory and Observation in Science", substantive revision 2026: https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/science-theory-observation/
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, "Scientific Method": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-method/

=== Justification ===

'''Capture definition:''' epistemic property or relation concerning whether a belief is appropriately supported, rational, warranted, or held on adequate grounds. Competing theories disagree about what makes justification obtain.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* propositional justification vs doxastic justification;
* justification ≠ truth;
* justification ≠ knowledge;
* reasons/evidence possessed vs reasons actually basing the belief;
* internalist/externalist accounts;
* foundationalist/coherentist/reliabilist/evidentialist approaches.

'''High-value sources:'''
* SEP, "Internalist vs. Externalist Conceptions of Epistemic Justification": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/justep-intext/
* SEP, "The Epistemic Basing Relation": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/basing-epistemic/
* SEP foundationalist/coherentist/reliabilist entries above.

== Cluster B — calibration and lineage ==

=== Uncertainty ===

'''Capture definition:''' limited or incomplete determination about a state, value, proposition, model, outcome, or future event. Different domains distinguish epistemic uncertainty, aleatory variability, measurement uncertainty, model uncertainty, semantic/vagueness uncertainty, and other forms.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* uncertainty ≠ error;
* uncertainty ≠ probability, though probability can represent some forms;
* epistemic vs aleatory;
* reducible vs irreducible as a context-sensitive distinction;
* measurement uncertainty has a metrological definition;
* uncertainty about a proposition vs uncertainty about a parameter/model.

'''High-value sources:'''
* NIST, "Measurement Uncertainty": https://www.nist.gov/itl/sed/topic-areas/measurement-uncertainty
* JCGM/BIPM VIM (measurement uncertainty definitions).
* Stanford Encyclopedia entries on Bayesian epistemology, probability interpretations, certainty, higher-order evidence.
* [[BITwiki:Uncertainty]] only as BITwiki-specific application.

=== Epistemic status ===

'''Capture definition:''' a general family term for the epistemic standing/properties of a belief, claim, proposition, or cognitive state—e.g. justified, warranted, known, certain, reliable, safe, sensitive, defeated/undefeated. There is no universal single ordered scale.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* epistemic status ≠ confidence;
* epistemic status ≠ truth value;
* epistemic status ≠ lifecycle state;
* epistemic status ≠ BITwiki's local controlled vocabulary;
* outright belief statuses vs graded credence.

'''High-value sources:'''
* SEP, "Certainty": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/certainty/
* SEP, "Reliabilist Epistemology": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/reliabilism/
* SEP, "Metaepistemology" and formal/bayesian epistemology for belief/credence distinctions.
* Contemporary philosophical literature uses "epistemic status" as an umbrella for justification, warrant, safety, sensitivity, reliability, etc.; Core must say the term is broad and theory-relative.
* [[BITwiki:Epistemic status]] is a local implementation and should be linked only in an application section.

=== Provenance ===

'''Capture definition:''' information about the origin, custody, production, transformation, derivation, and responsible agents/activities associated with an object, record, data item, claim, or artifact.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* provenance ≠ citation;
* provenance ≠ authorship alone;
* provenance ≠ truth;
* physical-object/archival provenance vs digital/data provenance;
* provenance record vs actual history;
* provenance supports trust assessment but does not guarantee correctness.

'''High-value sources:'''
* W3C PROV Overview: https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-overview/
* W3C PROV Data Model/Ontology/Constraints/Primer: https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-primer/ ; https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/ ; https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-constraints/
* [[BITwiki:Provenance]] as a local knowledge-lineage application.

== Cluster C — checking and refutation ==

=== Verification ===

'''Capture definition:''' checking/confirming, by appropriate evidence or procedure, that specified requirements, properties, calculations, claims, implementations, or representations satisfy an applicable specification. Exact meaning varies across engineering, software, formal methods, science, and source checking.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* verification ≠ validation;
* verification ≠ truth in every domain;
* formal verification ≠ testing;
* source verification ≠ empirical replication;
* in systems engineering: "built right" shorthand is useful but not universal.

'''High-value sources:'''
* ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148 definitions of verification/validation.
* ISO 9000 family terminology (current edition exists; definitions vary by application).
* NASA Systems Engineering Handbook verification/validation sections.
* NIST modeling/simulation V&V literature.

=== Validation ===

'''Capture definition:''' evaluation/confirmation that something is adequate, fit, or valid for an intended use, target, application, interpretation, or empirical domain. Meaning is domain-specific.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* validation ≠ verification;
* model validation ≠ logical proof;
* construct/content/criterion validation in measurement/social science are specialized uses;
* data validation vs system validation vs scientific model validation;
* validation is not metaphysical certainty.

'''High-value sources:'''
* ISO/IEC/IEEE 29148: validation as evidence that requirements for a specific intended use/application are fulfilled.
* NASA Systems Engineering Handbook.
* NIST model verification/validation literature, including CFAST and simulation-model papers.
* [[BITwiki:Claim validation]] as a local knowledge-validation workflow, not the general definition.

=== Falsifiability ===

'''Capture definition:''' capacity of a statement/theory to conflict with some possible observation or basic statement; historically central to Popper's demarcation account. Logical falsifiability must be distinguished from the practical/methodological act of falsification.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* falsifiable ≠ false;
* unfalsified ≠ verified;
* logical counterinstance vs real-world observation with measurement/auxiliary assumptions;
* Popperian demarcation is influential but not universally accepted as a complete account of science;
* degrees/testability and auxiliary hypotheses.

'''High-value sources:'''
* SEP, "Scientific Method": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-method/
* SEP, "Karl Popper".
* SEP, "Science and Pseudo-Science": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/pseudo-science/
* Karl Popper, ''The Logic of Scientific Discovery''.

=== Counterexample ===

'''Capture definition:''' a case satisfying a claim's hypotheses/conditions while making its conclusion false; one valid counterexample refutes a universal claim.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* counterexample ≠ anecdote;
* counterexample must satisfy the hypotheses;
* contrary instance to a probabilistic/statistical claim does not necessarily refute it;
* counterexample can reveal an omitted condition and improve a conjecture;
* minimal counterexample is a proof technique, not just an example.

'''High-value sources:'''
* Wolfram MathWorld, "Counterexample": https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Counterexample.html
* OpenStax, ''Introduction to Philosophy'', logical statements/counterexamples.
* D. J. Velleman, ''How to Prove It'', 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press (2019).
* Imre Lakatos, ''Proofs and Refutations''.

== Cluster D — scientific practice and modeling ==

=== Scientific method ===

'''Capture definition:''' family of systematic practices by which scientific inquiry generates, tests, revises, compares, and applies empirical/theoretical claims. There is no single fixed sequence shared by all sciences.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* scientific method ≠ one universal step list;
* discovery vs justification/testing;
* observation, experiment, modeling, measurement, inference, hypothesis, theory;
* induction/deduction/abduction;
* pluralism and domain variation;
* methodological norms vs sociological institutions.

'''High-value sources:'''
* SEP, "Scientific Method", substantive revision 2026: https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-method/
* National Academies / field-methodology sources as domain examples.
* Popper is one historical approach, not the complete definition.

=== Scientific model ===

'''Capture definition:''' a representation used in science to describe, explain, predict, explore, simulate, infer about, or understand a target system, phenomenon, data set, or theory.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* model ≠ theory;
* model ≠ simulation;
* model ≠ data;
* physical, conceptual, mathematical, computational, statistical models;
* idealization/abstraction;
* model-to-target relation;
* models can be explanatory or useful without literal similarity.

'''High-value sources:'''
* SEP, "Models in Science": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/models-science/
* Modeling/simulation verification and validation literature from NIST.
* Technical sections should distinguish model verification, calibration, validation, sensitivity, and uncertainty.

=== Measurement ===

'''Capture definition:''' process/activity of obtaining a representation/result concerning a property or quantity through interaction, comparison, modeling, calibration, and reference systems; philosophical and metrological definitions emphasize different aspects.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* indication/reading ≠ measurement result;
* measurand ≠ instrument reading;
* measurement ≠ observation generally;
* calibration ≠ measurement;
* value + unit + uncertainty/context;
* theory/model dependence of measurement.

'''High-value sources:'''
* SEP, "Measurement in Science": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/measurement-science/
* JCGM VIM3 / BIPM vocabulary.
* NIST measurement uncertainty and TN 1297.

=== Accuracy and precision ===

'''Capture definition:''' measurement accuracy concerns closeness between measured value and reference/true quantity value; precision concerns agreement among replicate measurements under specified conditions. They are related but distinct.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* accuracy ≠ precision;
* precision does not imply trueness/accuracy;
* accuracy is qualitative in VIM3 and should not casually be assigned a numerical "accuracy" value;
* precision depends on specified repeatability/reproducibility conditions;
* uncertainty ≠ simply "accuracy + precision".

'''High-value sources:'''
* BIPM/JCGM VIM3, measurement accuracy: https://jcgm.bipm.org/vim/en/2.13.html
* BIPM/JCGM VIM3, measurement precision: https://jcgm.bipm.org/vim/en/2.15.html
* NIST TN 1297 terminology: https://www.nist.gov/pml/nist-technical-note-1297/nist-tn-1297-appendix-d1-terminology
* Note: VIM4 is under development; Core should cite published VIM3 definitions and mention vocabulary evolution only if necessary.

=== Reproducibility ===

'''Capture definition:''' term with field-dependent meanings. For Wave 1 Core, explicitly adopt the National Academies 2019 distinction as one influential modern convention: computational reproducibility means obtaining consistent computational results using the same data, code/methods, and conditions.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* reproducibility terminology varies across metrology, experimental science, computation, and journals;
* NASEM reproducibility ≠ replication;
* reproducibility ≠ correctness;
* transparent artifacts/provenance enable reproducibility but do not guarantee scientific validity;
* in metrology "reproducibility conditions" has a different established technical meaning.

'''High-value sources:'''
* National Academies, ''Reproducibility and Replicability in Science'' (2019): https://nap.nationalacademies.org/catalog/25303/reproducibility-and-replicability-in-science
* NIST/VIM for measurement reproducibility terminology.
* W3C PROV for provenance/reproducibility relation.

=== Replication ===

'''Capture definition:''' repeat investigation using new data/observations/study execution aimed at the same or closely related scientific question; exact methodology and terminology vary by field.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* replication ≠ computational reproduction;
* direct/exact vs conceptual replication labels are field-dependent;
* failure to replicate can have many causes and is evidence requiring interpretation, not automatic disproof;
* replication studies test robustness/generalization under changed samples or conditions.

'''High-value sources:'''
* National Academies 2019 report: replicability = consistent results across studies addressing the same scientific question with new data.
* Methodological literature should be added in Technical as needed.

=== Explanation ===

'''Capture definition:''' an account that makes something intelligible by showing why/how it occurs, holds, or follows. In science, explanations may appeal to causes, mechanisms, laws, models, unification, statistics, or pragmatic relevance.

'''Required distinctions:'''
* explanation ≠ description;
* explanation ≠ prediction;
* explanation ≠ understanding, though strongly related;
* causal explanation is not the only proposed form;
* scientific explanation literature has competing models.

'''High-value sources:'''
* SEP, "Scientific Explanation": https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/scientific-explanation/
* Hempel & Oppenheim, "Studies in the Logic of Explanation" (1948).
* Salmon, Kitcher, van Fraassen, mechanistic and causal explanation traditions.
* SEP "Models in Science" for model/explanation relation.

