== Source-family audit ==

=== Wikipedia ===
Use for title normalization, redirects, historical orientation, "see also" discovery, and public terminology. Do not use it as the principal evidentiary authority where primary/standards/SEP sources are available.

=== Scholarpedia ===
Direct general coverage of these broad epistemology/metrology concepts is sparse. Specialized Scholarpedia articles may become useful later for specific technical descendants. Do not manufacture coverage by counting unrelated same-word articles.

=== Google Scholar / scholarly discovery ===
Use as discovery for foundational papers and monographs, but resolve citations to publisher, DOI, journal, standards body, institutional report, or canonical author version before final article use.

=== Standards and institutional sources ===
* ISO/IEC/IEEE — verification/validation/system engineering vocabulary.
* BIPM/JCGM — metrology, measurement, accuracy, precision, uncertainty.
* NIST — measurement uncertainty, metrology, modeling/simulation V&V.
* W3C PROV — digital provenance.
* National Academies — reproducibility/replicability terminology.

=== Philosophy sources ===
SEP is especially valuable for Knowledge, Belief, Evidence, Justification, Epistemology, Scientific method, Models in Science, Measurement, Scientific Explanation, Popper/falsifiability, Bayesian epistemology, and certainty.

== Phase-1 unresolved decisions ==

# Should [[Epistemic status]] remain a public standalone concept or redirect later to a broader [[Epistemic evaluation]] / [[Epistemic properties]] page? For Wave 1, retain it provisionally because the phrase has real philosophical use, but explicitly avoid universal ladders.
# Should [[Reproducibility]] Core adopt NASEM terminology as the primary convention? Yes, but label it as a convention and document field variation.
# Should [[Verification]] and [[Validation]] have broad general definitions or engineering-first definitions? Broad Core, with engineering/software/modeling distinctions in dedicated sections.
# Should [[Explanation]] be "Scientific explanation"? Keep [[Explanation]] because the concept is public and cross-domain; scientific explanation is the dominant technical literature within this wave.
# Should [[Accuracy and precision]] remain one page? Yes for public clarity, with redirects from future [[Accuracy]] and [[Precision]] where appropriate.

== Phase-1 completion criteria ==

Before Wave 1 advances to compilation:

# every concept has a normalized title and non-equivalence boundaries;
# the epistemic core is defined before downstream science/method concepts;
# standards-specific meanings are marked as domain-specific;
# BITwiki-internal frameworks are separated from public definitions;
# source dossiers contain authoritative or foundational material, not citation-count proxies;
# cross-wave links are recorded;
# unresolved terminology differences remain explicit rather than being silently collapsed.

